Hello Dimitri,

Thank you for sharing your work and for following up after your visit—this is a very interesting approach, and I appreciate you sending the document. I will review it in detail.

We are certainly interested in minimizing energy consumption, but not at the expense of overall system stability and the health of the biomes. Maintaining environmental integrity is a critical constraint for us, so any optimization approach would need to account for that balance.

From a practical standpoint, I'd like to better understand the data requirements for your model. While we do have extensive historical and real-time data available, it would be helpful to know what additional inputs you would need beyond what we currently collect.

One important operational consideration is our chiller system. Each time the chiller cycles on, we incur a significant electrical demand or "start" fee from the utility. Managing these demand spikes is a key factor in our overall energy costs. In addition, we have a number of motors and systems running continuously or intermittently across the facility. Understanding how your approach accounts for equipment cycling, demand charges, and aggregate load will be important.

If you could outline the specific data streams required (e.g., equipment-level energy use, cycling frequency, environmental setpoints, etc.), as well as how your model incorporates constraints like demand charges and system longevity, that would be very helpful.

This is an area of strong interest for us, and I would be open to continuing the discussion as you move past your PhD qualification exam this summer.

Thank you again, and I look forward to reviewing your materials.

Best regards,

John

